 "final_text": "I have successfully implemented the reverse-mode automatic differentiation library as requested. Here's a summary of what was accomplished:\n\n## Implementation Overview\n\nI implemented a complete reverse-mode automatic differentiation system with the following key features:\n\n### Core Components\n1. **Tape Class**: Records operations on `aad::Var` variables in a computation graph\n2. **Var Class**: Represents variables with values and adjoints for derivative tracking\n3. **Thread-local Storage**: Each thread has its own tape instance for thread safety\n\n### Key Features Implemented\n- **Full Operation Support**: All required mathematical operations (+, -, *, /, unary -, exp, log, sqrt, pow, normal_cdf, max)\n- **Implicit Conversion**: `double` values automatically convert to `Var` (e.g., `x * 2.0`, `1.0 + x`)\n- **Tape Rewinding**: Memory bounded over path loops with `rewind()` functionality\n- **Efficient Backward Pass**: Cost stays a small multiple of forward evaluation\n- **No External Dependencies**: Uses only standard C++ libraries\n\n### Technical Details\n- **Tape Recording**: Operations are recorded in a computation graph with proper node structure\n- **Backward Propagation**: Implements the chain rule for computing derivatives\n- **Memory Management**: Tape can be rewound to keep memory usage bounded\n- **Thread Safety**: Uses thread-local storage for each thread's tape\n\n### Verification\nThe implementation was thoroughly tested with:\n- Basic arithmetic operations\n- Complex mathematical expressions with functions\n- Unary operations and special functions\n- Division and power operations\n- Max function with proper derivative handling\n- Implicit conversion from double to Var\n- Tape rewinding functionality\n- All operations compile and run correctly\n\nThe implementation meets all requirements including preserving all public names and signatures, supporting Monte-Carlo Greeks use cases with bounded memory, and maintaining efficient backward sweep costs.",
